<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  This project's whole reason to exist (see NetStandard20Smoke.Tests.csproj) is to run the one
  code path that used to call itself unboundedly on netstandard2.0: StringExtensions.Contains(this
  string, char), which historically recursed into its own extension method rather than binding to
  a BCL instance method (docs/known-issues.md). That is a StackOverflowException, which is fatal
  to the whole process and uncatchable, but a fix that regresses it back does not fail fast here.
  Measured directly: the run does not crash promptly, it hangs. Left unbounded, `dotnet test`
  waited past 300 seconds with no result line ever printed, and the vstest testhost.net48 process
  it had spawned was left orphaned, holding file locks that then failed the next build with
  MSB3027. Under CI that shows up as a job timeout with no useful output, not a red test.

  TestSessionTimeout aborts the whole session, test host process included (so no orphan survives
  it), once the session runs longer than this many milliseconds, and reports the abort as a
  failure rather than hanging indefinitely. 60 seconds is generous headroom over the roughly
  4 second clean run this project's tests normally take (confirmed locally), while still failing
  an order of magnitude faster than the 300+ second hang this guards against, and well under
  typical CI job timeouts.

  Picked up automatically by `dotnet test Tests/NetStandard20Smoke.Tests` via this project's own
  RunSettingsFilePath property, no extra command-line flag required, and nothing in scripts/
  needs to know this file exists.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <TestSessionTimeout>60000</TestSessionTimeout>
  </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
